WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.787
Task statement 3.3.

2
00:00:01.787 --> 00:00:05.918
Apply path-specific rules for conditional convention loading.

3
00:00:05.918 --> 00:00:13.705
Test files sit beside source code in forty folders; one path-scoped rule teaches Claude your test style everywhere.

4
00:00:15.105 --> 00:00:17.791
Not every convention matters for every task.

5
00:00:17.791 --> 00:00:21.514
Your test style only matters when Claude touches a test file.

6
00:00:21.514 --> 00:00:27.618
Path specific rules let you attach instructions to file patterns, so they appear only when relevant.

7
00:00:27.618 --> 00:00:32.257
Think of a shop sign that lights up only when you walk into the right aisle.

8
00:00:33.657 --> 00:00:39.249
Create a markdown file in the dot claude rules folder and add frontmatter with a paths list.

9
00:00:39.249 --> 00:00:41.012
Each entry is a glob pattern.

10
00:00:41.012 --> 00:00:48.427
Two stars followed by a slash match any number of folders, so a pattern like that catches test files anywhere in the tree.

11
00:00:48.427 --> 00:00:52.985
A rule file with no paths field simply loads at the start of every session.

12
00:00:54.385 --> 00:00:57.078
Both approaches target part of the codebase.

13
00:00:57.078 --> 00:01:02.219
A CLAUDE dot md inside a folder is great when the convention belongs to that folder.

14
00:01:02.219 --> 00:01:10.909
But when the same convention applies to files scattered everywhere, like tests next to source code, a single path rule with a glob is cleaner.

15
00:01:10.909 --> 00:01:13.969
One source of truth, loaded only where it applies.

16
00:01:15.369 --> 00:01:22.651
In the developer productivity scenario, an engineer asks Claude to add a feature in one package and a test beside it.

17
00:01:22.651 --> 00:01:27.008
When Claude opens the test file, the testing rule loads automatically.

18
00:01:27.008 --> 00:01:31.552
When another engineer edits a config file, the rule stays out of the way.

19
00:01:31.552 --> 00:01:34.975
The convention has one home and the context stays lean.

20
00:01:36.375 --> 00:01:45.294
When the question describes a convention for a kind of file spread across many directories, the answer is a path scoped rule with a recursive glob.

21
00:01:45.294 --> 00:01:48.085
Copying rules into many folders creates drift.

22
00:01:48.085 --> 00:01:50.816
Putting them in the root file wastes context.

23
00:01:50.816 --> 00:01:55.852
And check the glob itself: a pattern without the double star misses nested folders.

24
00:01:57.252 --> 00:02:00.176
Path specific rules are conditional instructions.

25
00:02:00.176 --> 00:02:07.934
Put them in the dot claude rules folder, give them a paths list of globs, and they load only when Claude works on a matching file.

26
00:02:07.934 --> 00:02:14.319
Reach for them when a convention follows a file type across the codebase, rather than living in one folder.

